    "body": "Part of the CI/CD plan on #2203. Closes #2456.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Replaced the one serial job with three independent ones.** `Build and Deploy Hazel` was a\n  single 21\u201331.5 min job that published the preview and *then* ran the tests \u2014 one check covering\n  everything, with the test result buried after a deploy that had already happened. Now\n  `Fast checks`, `Full tests` and `Deploy branch preview` are three jobs with no `needs:` between\n  them. Each re-runs alone, so a flaky suite costs one job rather than another deploy and another\n  30 minutes, and the fast signal comes back in ~4 minutes instead of at the end of the full run.\n\n- **Turned off `setup-ocaml`'s dune cache and made the opam switch cache single-writer.** Its\n  entries are keyed on the run id, so they never hit, and 10.24 GiB of them were evicting the\n  `_opam` switch from a 10 GiB budget. The switch cache is now `restore` + `save` with\n  `dev`/`bot-update-deps` as the only writers, plus `restore-keys` for a warm start after a\n  lockfile bump.\n\n  Caching the switch ourselves is deliberate and unsupported: `setup-ocaml` declines to do it\n  (ocaml/setup-ocaml#1098), on the grounds that cached dependencies drift from opam-repository.\n  We take that trade knowingly \u2014 it moves `Install dependencies` from 3\u20137.5 min to ~6 s, which is\n  what sets how long we wait for a deploy and for static-check feedback \u2014 and the lock file bounds\n  the risk. It doesn't eliminate it: in-place edits to already-published packages have broken us\n  before (#2334).\n- **Added `make ci-check` to enforce release warnings on `test/`** (#2456). `dune build @check\n  --profile release` type-checks both trees without linking \u2014 21 s, no second build of the test\n  bundle. Warnings in `test/` previously printed and passed.\n- **Made test failures produce a non-zero exit code.** Alcotest is run with `and_exit:false` so\n  the JUnit report can be written first, and junit_alcotest hands the outcome back as a function\n  to call afterwards \u2014 the only thing that propagates a failure. `test/haz3ltest.re` discarded\n  it, so the test binary exited 0 whatever happened, and every entry point keyed on that exit\n  code passed on a red suite: `dune runtest`, `make ci`, `make test`, `make test-quick` and\n  `./run_tests`.\n- **Published the test reports as check runs** (`use-actions-summary: false`). The default writes\n  results to the run's job summary and creates no check run, so `Test Report` never appeared in a\n  PR's check list \u2014 nothing to click through to, no inline annotations on failing tests, and\n  nothing that could be named as a required status on `dev`.\n\n## What runs where\n\n```\nchecks.yml   \u2500\u2500 on: workflow_call                       the definitions, one copy\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Fast checks       fmt \u2192 make ci-check \u2192 make ci-quick \u2192 Quick Test Report\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Full tests        make ci \u2192 Test Report \u2192 Codecov\n\nci.yml       \u2500\u2500 on: push, except gh-readonly-queue/** | merge_group\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 calls checks.yml\n\nfork-ci.yml  \u2500\u2500 on: pull_request, if the pull request comes from a fork\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 calls checks.yml, with the report and coverage steps off\n\ndeploy.yml   \u2500\u2500 on: push, except gh-readonly-queue/**\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Deploy branch preview    release build \u2192 hazel.org/build/<branch>\n```\n\nEvery filter is a trigger rather than an `if:`, which leaves **two conditionals in the whole\nsystem**: the fork test, and one input that turns off the report check runs and the Codecov\nupload for forks, whose `GITHUB_TOKEN` is read-only and which get no secrets.\n\n**`ci.yml` has no `pull_request` trigger on purpose.** A same-repo pull request fires both it and\n`push` for the same commit, so the full suite would run twice. Forks are the only case `push`\ncannot see \u2014 a fork's pushes happen in the fork \u2014 and `fork-ci.yml` covers them.\n\n**Fork PRs gain CI.** Today's `deploy_branches.yml` is `on: [push]`, so fork PRs get no checks at\nall; 3 of the last 200 PRs here came from forks. They now get `Fast checks` and `Full tests`,\nwithout the report check runs their token can't create. They still get no branch preview.\n\n**No merge queue exists yet**, but both `ci.yml` triggers are load-bearing once one does. A queue\npushes its `gh-readonly-queue/**` branch, yet Actions only reports status into the queue from a\n`merge_group` run \u2014 so the trigger is what makes the queue's checks exist, and `branches-ignore`\nis what stops the same commit being checked twice and a speculative merge being published as a\npreview. Turning the queue on is #2203 phase 4.\n\n**Why formatting, warnings and the quick suite share a job.** Every job installs OCaml and the\ndependencies independently, ~2 min warm and 5\u20139 min cold, which is more than those three checks\ncost to run. Splitting them would buy precise names and precise retry for roughly double the\nwall clock. Worth revisiting after the build-once artifact work in the follow-ups, which is what\nmakes extra jobs cheap.\n\n`Build release` is no longer a separate gate \u2014 the deploy job runs the same command. That leaves\n`src/web`'s `main` and `worker` jsoo executables unlinked on fork PRs and in the merge queue,\nwhich we're accepting until it causes a problem; `Full tests` still links the `web` library\nthrough the test bundle.\n\n## Notes for merge\n\n- **`make test` and `./run_tests` now fail on a red suite.** That's the point, but it may surface\n  failures that have been passing silently across the open PRs \u2014 #2128's evaluator/stepper QCheck\n  flake most likely.\n- The strict formatting gate wasn't in the original plan and can turn other branches red. Easy to\n  drop.\n- **The warm-cache win can't be demonstrated until this reaches `dev`.** Only `dev` and\n  `bot-update-deps` write the switch cache, so the first push after merging populates\n  `Linux-opam-*` and the *second* is where `Install dependencies` should fall to single digits.\n- **Branch protection and the merge queue are both future work** (#2203 phase 4). When you set\n  them up, the required checks are named `CI / checks / Fast checks` and `CI / checks / Full\n  tests` \u2014 the reusable-workflow nesting makes those non-obvious, so it's noted in `ci.yml` too.\n  The `merge_group` path is the one thing here that can't be exercised until a queue exists.\n- ~~Also pins the opam repository, resolving #2334.~~ Reverted in bd8c01b3b2 \u2014 a separate concern\n  that made this harder to review. #2334 stays open and gets its own PR.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
